questions.explanation:
## Why This Is Asked
This question tests precise bit-manipulation skills across 128-bit data without loops or branches. It validates the ability to split work across halves and combine results safely while demonstrating understanding of efficient parallel counting algorithms.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit values represented as two 64-bit halves
- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register) popcount on 64-bit words
- Combining two 64-bit counts to produce a 0-128 result
- Bitmask-based parallel computation

## Code Example
```c
uint64_t popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  return x & 0x3FULL;
}

uint64_t popcount128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  return popcount64(hi) + popcount64(lo);
}
```

## Examples
- `popcount128(0x8000000000000000ULL, 0x0000000000000001ULL)` returns 2 (bits at positions 127 and 0)
- `popcount128(0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL)` returns 128 (all bits set)

The SWAR algorithm processes all 64 bits in parallel using bitmasks to accumulate counts efficiently.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests per-byte parallel popcount without loops, scales to 128-bit data, and checks careful bit-twiddling across halves.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR per-byte popcount
- 128-bit lane handling via hi/lo halves
- No branches, fixed sequence of masks/shifts

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 countBytes128(uint128 x) {
  uint64_t a = x.lo;
  uint64_t b = x.hi;
  a = a - ((a >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  a = (a & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((a >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  a = (a + (a >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  b = b - ((b >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  b = (b & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((b >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  b = (b + (b >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  uint128 r = { .hi = b, .lo = a };
  return r;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to count bits in arbitrary 16-byte vectors with fewer operations? 
- What are the effects of endianness on byte ordering in different compilers and architectures?

## Why This Is Asked
This evaluates your ability to extend bit manipulation techniques to multi-register data structures while maintaining branchless performance.

## Key Concepts
- Two's complement negation for 64-bit words
- Isolating least significant set bit with x & -x
- Branchless masking using arithmetic on boolean predicates

## Code Example
```javascript
void isolateLeastBit128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  uint64_t lo_lsb = lo & -lo;
  uint64_t hi_lsb = hi & -hi;
  uint64_t nonzero_lo = -(lo != 0);
  uint64_t zero_lo = -(lo == 0);
  *out_lo = lo_lsb & nonzero_lo;
  *out_hi = hi_lsb & zero_lo;
}
```

## Example
Input: hi=0x8000, lo=0x0C00 → Output: out_hi=0x0000, out_lo=0x0400
Input: hi=0x0000, lo=0x0000 → Output: out_hi=0x0000, out_lo=0x0000

## Why This Is Asked
3D Morton codes enable fast spatial indexing in grid-like domains; a branchless, fixed-bit spreading approach scales predictably. 

## Key Concepts
- Morton codes for 3D require interleaving bits from three coordinates.
- Bit spreading (dilating) inserts two zero bits between each original bit; a fixed sequence of masks/shifts yields a branchless implementation.
- BigInt vs. Number in JavaScript: use BigInt for 64-bit results if implementing in JS; otherwise 64-bit types in C/C++ handle it natively.

## Code Example
```javascript
function part1by2(n) {
  let x = BigInt(n) & 0x1fffffn; // 21 bits
  x = (x | (x << 32n)) & 0x1f00000000ffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 16n)) & 0x1f0000ff0000ffn;
  x = (x | (x << 8n)) & 0x100f00f00f00f00fn;
  x = (x | (x << 4n)) & 0x10c30c30c30c30c3n;
  x = (x | (x << 2n)) & 0x1249249249249249n;
  return x;
}
function morton3D(x, y, z) {
  return part1by2(x) | (part1by2(y) << 1n) | (part1by2(z) << 2n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify correctness for random inputs? 
- How to adapt to larger coordinates or alternative word sizes?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design a branchless, constant-time population-count across 128 bits using only shifts and masks, a common low-level optimization in crypto/signature code.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR (SIMD Within A Register)
- 64-bit popcount, extended to 128-bit
- Branchless arithmetic for performance

## Code Example

```c
unsigned int popcount64(uint64_t w) {
  w = w - ((w >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  w = (w & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((w >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  w = (w + (w >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  return (unsigned int)((w * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56);
}
unsigned int popcount128(uint128 x) {
  unsigned int hi = popcount64(x.hi);
  unsigned int lo = popcount64(x.lo);
  return hi + lo;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for variable-width counts (e.g., 256-bit)?
- What are the trade-offs compared to using hardware popcnt when available?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to extend known 64-bit bit tricks to 128-bit values without branches. Emphasizes portability and optimization.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR parallel bit counting
- Masks and shifts across 64-bit lanes
- Combining results from hi/lo halves

## Code Example
```javascript
static inline unsigned popcount64(uint64_t x) {
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL;
  x = x + (x >> 8);
  x = x + (x >> 16);
  x = x + (x >> 32);
  return (unsigned)(x & 0x7F);
}
unsigned countBits128(uint128 x) {
  return popcount64(x.hi) + popcount64(x.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you use hardware intrinsics if allowed?
- How would you extend to 256-bit types or streaming data?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to perform endianness reversal across a 128-bit value with no branches.

## Key Concepts
- 128-bit layout via hi/lo; byte-level reversal; pure bit tricks.
- 64-bit byte swap implementation using shifts/masks.
- Branchless data movement across two halves.

## Code Example
```c
static inline uint64_t bswap64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32) | ((x & 0xFFFFFFFF00000000ULL) >> 32);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x & 0xFFFF0000FFFF0000ULL) >> 16);
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8)  | ((x & 0xFF00FF00FF00FF00ULL) >> 8);
  return x;
}
void swapEndianness128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo){
  *out_hi = bswap64(lo);
  *out_lo = bswap64(hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this to a 256-bit type? (Explain any performance considerations.)
- Could you implement a generic macro to swap endianness for any multiple of 8 bytes?
